Description: fix frame leaks and timer starvation in the whisper framehook
 The whisper framehook added upstream in "Audiohooks: Allow whisper audiohooks
 to work without an underlying stream" (first shipped in 22.11.0) leaks two
 frames per timer driven read and can leave its timer fd permanently readable.
 .
 ast_write() does not take ownership of the frame it is handed, so the silent
 frame the framehook injects was only freed when the write failed. The callback
 also returned &ast_null_frame while discarding the frame it was given;
 framehook_list_push_event() does not free the original, that is the callback's
 job. Both are AST_FRAME_VOICE frames, so each leak holds a reference on the
 cached slin format and eventually trips the ao2 excessive refcount assertion
 in __ast_frdup(). The discarded frame is also a real read frame the whisper
 audiohooks were already mixed into, so audio was being dropped.
 .
 The timer was also only acknowledged when the read event saw
 ast_channel_fdno() equal to the framehook's own fd slot.
 ast_waitfor_nandfds() records one fd per channel per wakeup and lets later
 channels override earlier ones, so a caller waiting on several channels
 routinely reads the hooked channel with fdno pointing elsewhere. The timer fd
 is level triggered, so a skipped ack leaves it readable forever and every
 later ast_waitfor() on that channel returns immediately, spinning the caller
 at 100% CPU. The DAHDI and pthread timing modules make this permanent by
 clearing only one expiration per ack; timerfd drains all of them, which is why
 it only reproduces on some systems.
 .
 app_rpt is the only whisper audiohook user in ASL: it attaches one to l->pchan
 for every link, so this hits nodes as soon as an ilink connects.
